   If I understand your problem, you do a dceping -C on your machine
and it says that it can't find the cds server, and it asks you for the
server name.  You type it in and dceping then works fine.

   This is a minor problem but has nothing to do with the actual
operation of DCE.  What happens is that dceping stores the name of the 
CDS server in a file in /tmp (it should be semi-obvious).  If your root
cron script cleans old files out of /tmp, then the file gets wiped and 
dceping looses its mind.  But in reality your DCE cell is fine.  
(If dceval fails its tests, then I worry.)

   I got badly burned by this mis-feature when I put dceping -C into a
cron script.  Dceping went looking for input from stdin, which was not 
to be had from my cron script, so it send the script into an endless
query loop that burned mucho CPU time and brought the system to its
knees.  Of course the cron script kicked off at 2 AM...

