Jonathan Nicholson <jjn@sanger.ac.uk> writes:

> byron[root]4: setld -i | grep -i micro
> MMEDRVMSB220         installed  Microsoft Sound Board Device Support
> V2.2(Hardware Option Support for PCI/EISA/ISA Bus Machines)
> 
> I still can't get it to work, the documentation is sketchy at best, and in
> most cases down right non-existant!

You need to build a new kernel with the line

controller      msb0    at * vector msbintr

in the kernel configuration, and boot the new kernel.  You also need to
enable the MME server, which requires installing the Multimedia Runtime
services.  Here's what I have installed on my system besides the device
support:

setld -i | grep -i 'installed.*multimedia'
MMEDOC220            installed  Multimedia Services V2.2 for Digital UNIX Documentation(Documentation Kit)
MMEDOCHW220          installed  Multimedia Services V2.2 for Digital UNIX Hardware Option Documentation(Documentation Kit)
MMEMANRT220          installed  Multimedia Services V2.2 for Digital UNIX Runtime Man Pages(Run-Time Kit)
MMERELNOTES220       installed  Multimedia Services V2.2 for Digital UNIX Release Notes(Documentation Kit)
MMERT220             installed  Multimedia Services V2.2 for Digital UNIX Runtime(Run-Time Kit)
MMERTCDE220          installed  Multimedia Services V2.2 for Digital UNIX CDE Integration(Run-Time Kit)
MMERTSMPLDAT220      installed  Multimedia Services V2.2 for Digital UNIX Sample Data(Run-Time Kit)

It even includes documentation.

