content-type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
organization: Digital Reflections
mime-version: 1.0
newsgroups: comp.graphics.api.opengl,comp.sys.dec,comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.unix.osf.misc
x-mailer: Mozilla 3.01 (X11; I; SunOS 5.5 sun4m)


A few days ago I posted:

> I just tried installing DEC Open3D 420 on my DEC 3000/300LX (with
> standard HX graphics) running Digital UNIX 4.0. The subsets all
> installed fine, and the GLX X server extension seems to be present,
> but running xglinfo reports that *NONE* of the visuals support OpenGL.

Well, after a couple of hours of trial-and-error testing of various
changes to Xserver.conf, I managed to get it working :-)

I'm using the field-test version of Open3D (O3D420.tar) from 
http://www.service.digital.com/open3d/.

It's documentation says that for the 3D10 (which uses a software-only
implementation) you should add the line:
    < _dec_sampleGL lib_dec_sampleGL.so  __glXExtensionInit S3TRIO >

But this just produces an X server core dump for me.

The O3D420 docs also suggest changing the line:
    < _dec_opengl   lib_dec_opengl.so       GLExtensionInit p00041011 >
to: < _dec_sampleGL lib_dec_sampleGL.so  __glXExtensionInit p00041011 >

But that didn't do anything at all.

However, that got me thinking... I assume that the last argument in the
above lines (S3TRIO or p00041011) somehow identifies the hardware device
(presumably the S3TRIO is the 3D10, and I gather from the O3D420 docs
that the p00041011 is the ZLXp-E1).

So I did some trial-and-error tests, and after quite a few trials I
discovered that the HX must be the PMAGB-BA). Changing just the PMAGB-BA
line to use sampleGL worked!

So in summary, to get OpenGL (the O3D420 field test version) working on
the HX, all that is necessary is to change the line:
    < _dec_opengl   lib_dec_opengl.so       GLExtensionInit PMAGB-BA >
to...
    < _dec_sampleGL lib_dec_sampleGL.so __glXExtensionInit PMAGB-BA >

Then kill and restart the X server.


JASON PATTERSON
 jason@reflections.com.au  http://www.reflections.com.au/~jason

