Subject: Re: Why doesn't DFS support SETUID files?
Date: 1996/12/03
Message-ID: <vdk9qzmc6c.fsf@dce-1.wayne.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 202182232
references: <vd3exw8o8s.fsf@dce-1.wayne.edu> <vd20df8pg0.fsf@dce-1.wayne.edu>
organization: Wayne State University
newsgroups: comp.soft-sys.dce,comp.unix.osf.misc,comp.unix.osf.osf1


jmr@risc.sps.mot.com (Jim Rowan) writes:

> 
> 
> Let me correct you a bit:
> 
> Thomas> Some more information on the `cm setsetuid` DCE command. 
> [...]
> Thomas> the client machine. The `cm setsetuid` DCE command does not change
> Thomas> the settings of the SETUID flag for a given file, it only allows the
> Thomas> `chmod` command the permission to set thus flag within the cache
> Thomas> manager data for the given file. 
> 
> Umm, this isn't quite correct.  What it does is to control whether the setuid
> bit is honored for files (in this particular fileset).  It doesn't control
> whether chmod +s can be done.
> 
> Further -- the path given as an argument to cm setsetuid only serves to
> identify a fileset; the flag applies to a whole fileset, not a file.
> 
> [...]
> Thomas> Thus, after executing the `cm setsetuid` DCE command for a given
> Thomas> file, the root user must still execute a `chmod u+s` command to turn
> Thomas> on the SETUID flag for a given file. 
> 
> It doesn't have to be root, any user could do this.  (Only root can do it to
> other people's files...).  (Also, it can happen before or after the cache
> manager is told to honor setuid in that fileset.)

Thanks for the correction. I was getting confused by some of the
warning messages I was seeing. When I did the `chmod u+s` command to
the dfs files dsmapitca and dsmtca (before executing the `cm
setsetuid` command), I was getting the error message:

# chmod u+s dsmapitca dsmtca
0481-014 chmod: not all requested changes were made to dsmapitca
0481-014 chmod: not all requested changes were made to dsmtca

An `ls -al dsmapitca dsmtca` showed that the files still weren't
SETUID files:

# ls -al dsmapitca dsmtca
-rwxr-xr-x   1 root     system    276108 Sep  4 22:33 dsmapitca
-rwxr-xr-x   1 root     system    274124 Sep  4 22:33 dsmtca

This made me think I had to do the `cm setsetuid` command first before
executing the `chmod` command. Also the `cm help setsetuid` command
says:

# cm help setsetuid
cm setsetuid: set cell setuid status 
Usage: cm setsetuid  [-path {<filename> | <directory_name>}...] [-state {on | off}] [-help]

The "-path" option says filename or directory, even though it sets
everything for the entire fileset. This was also confusing to me.


